\section{Recursive Domain Equations}
\label{sec:recursive-domains}

\subsection{Solving Recursive Equations}
\label{sec:solving-recursive}

The general technique for solving recursive domain equations $D \cong F(D)$ involves:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Approximation}: Construct a chain of domains $D_0 \sqsubseteq D_1 \sqsubseteq D_2 \sqsubseteq \cdots$ where $D_0$ is a simple domain (e.g., $\bottom$) and $D_{n+1} = F(D_n)$.
  \item \textbf{Embedding--projection pairs}: Each inclusion $D_n \sqsubseteq D_{n+1}$ is represented by an embedding--projection pair $(e_n, p_n)$ where $e_n : D_n \to D_{n+1}$ and $p_n : D_{n+1} \to D_n$.
  \item \textbf{Limit}: The solution $D_\infty$ is the limit (colimit) of the chain, consisting of coherent sequences $(x_0, x_1, x_2, \ldots)$ where $x_n \in D_n$ and $p_n(x_{n+1}) = x_n$.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Scott's $D_\infty$ Model}
\label{sec:d-infinity}

Scott's $D_\infty$ model is the canonical solution of the domain equation $D \cong [D \to D]$:

\begin{definition}[Scott's $D_\infty$]
\label{def:d-infinity}
Define a sequence of domains:
\begin{align*}
  D_0 &= \bottom \\
  D_{n+1} &= [D_n \to D_n] \\
  D_\infty &= \lim_{\leftarrow} D_n \quad \text{(inverse limit)}
\end{align*}
Then $D_\infty \cong [D_\infty \to D_\infty]$.
\end{definition}

The $D_\infty$ model provides a mathematical model of the untyped $\lambda$-calculus, where every element is simultaneously a value and a function.

\section{Logical Relations}
\label{sec:logical-relations}

\subsection{Definition and Purpose}
\label{sec:logical-relations-def}

\begin{definition}[Logical Relation]
\label{def:logical-relation}
A \textbf{logical relation} on a type structure is a family of relations $\{R_\tau\}$ indexed by types $\tau$, such that for each type constructor, the relation at the constructed type is determined by the relations at the component types.
\end{definition}

Logical relations are used to prove properties of programming languages, including:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Definability}: Which elements of a model are definable by $\lambda$-terms?
  \item \textbf{Full abstraction}: Does the semantic model distinguish exactly the programs that can be distinguished by observations?
  \item \textbf{Parametricity}: What properties are guaranteed by parametric polymorphism?
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Logical Relations for Parametricity}
\label{sec:logical-relations-parametricity}

Reynolds's abstraction theorem (1983) uses logical relations to formalize the notion of parametric polymorphism:

\begin{theorem}[Abstraction Theorem]
\label{thm:abstraction}
If $\Gamma \vdash e : \tau$, then for every logical relation $R$, the interpretation of $e$ is related to itself by $R_\tau$.
\end{theorem}

This theorem implies that parametrically polymorphic functions cannot inspect their type arguments---they must behave uniformly for all types.

\section{Full Abstraction Results}
\label{sec:full-abstraction}

\subsection{The Full Abstraction Problem}
\label{sec:full-abstraction-problem}

\begin{definition}[Full Abstraction]
\label{def:full-abstraction}
A semantic model is \textbf{fully abstract} if semantic equivalence coincides with observational equivalence:
\[
\sembrack{M} = \sembrack{N} \iff M \simeq_{\mathrm{obs}} N
\]
\end{definition}

\subsection{PCF and Full Abstraction}
\label{sec:pcf-full-abstraction}

PCF (Programming language for Computable Functions, Plotkin 1977) is a simply-typed $\lambda$-calculus with natural numbers, conditionals, and a fixed-point combinator.

\begin{theorem}[Full Abstraction Failure for PCF]
\label{thm:pcf-failure}
The standard continuous function model of PCF is \emph{not} fully abstract. There exist semantically distinct elements that are observationally equivalent. (Plotkin, 1977; Sazonov, 1976)
\end{theorem}

The counterexample is the \emph{parallel or} function: a function $\texttt{por} : \Bool \to \Bool \to \Bool$ that returns $\texttt{true}$ if either argument is $\texttt{true}$, $\texttt{false}$ if both are $\texttt{false}$, and terminates (returning $\texttt{true}$) even if one argument diverges, as long as the other is $\texttt{true}$. This function is not definable in PCF but is continuous.

\begin{theorem}[Full Abstraction for PCF]
\label{thm:pcf-full-abs}
The game semantics model of PCF is fully abstract. (Abramsky, Jagadeesan, Malacaria, 1994; Hyland, Ong, 2000)
\end{theorem}

\section{Summary}
\label{sec:domains-summary}

Domain theory provides the mathematical foundation for denotational semantics. Its key contributions include:

\begin{itemize}
  \item CPOs and continuous functions as the basic mathematical structures for modeling computation.
  \item The fixed-point theorem as the foundation for interpreting recursion.
  \item Domain equations and their solutions for modeling recursive data types.
  \item Powerdomains for modeling nondeterminism.
  \item Logical relations for proving properties of programs and models.
  \item Full abstraction results connecting semantic models to observable behavior.
\end{itemize}

\begin{historical}
The development of domain theory from Scott's original work to modern applications spans over five decades. Key milestones include: Scott's $D_\infty$ model (1972); the fixed-point theorem and continuous functions (1970s); powerdomains (Plotkin 1976, Smyth 1978, Hoare 1979); the full abstraction problem for PCF (Plotkin 1977); Reynolds's abstraction theorem (1983); game semantics and full abstraction for PCF (Abramsky et al.\ 1994, Hyland and Ong 2000); and the ongoing development of domain theory in the context of synthetic domain theory and homotopy type theory.
\end{historical}

