\paragraph{Invariant and scope.}
Each loop covers a distinct universal obligation, so exhaustion
proves the two tables give an embedding--projection pair on
these domains. Testing all stages up to any chosen depth does
not prove that the infinite chain is well defined or that its
limit solves a domain equation. Moreover, on an infinite
domain, monotonicity alone no longer establishes continuity.

\subsection{Solutions for Other Domain Equations}
\label{sec:other-domain-eqs}

\begin{example}[Lazy Lists]
\label{ex:lazy-lists-domain}
The domain of lazy lists $L$ solves $L \cong 1 + A \times L$. Using the chain construction:
\begin{align*}
  D_0 &= \{\bot\} \\
  D_1 &= 1 + A \times \{\bot\} \cong 1 + A_\bot \\
  D_2 &= 1 + A \times (1 + A_\bot) \cong 1 + A + A \times A_\bot \\
  &\vdots
\end{align*}
The limit $D_\infty$ contains finite lists, infinite lists, and partial lists (with $\bot$ elements).
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Trees]
\label{ex:trees-domain}
The domain of binary trees $T$ solves $T \cong 1 + T \times T$. The solution includes finite trees, infinite trees, and partial trees.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Streams with State]
\label{ex:streams-state}
The domain of stateful streams $S$ solves $S \cong \Store \to (A \times S)_\bot$. This is a recursive domain equation involving the function space constructor, requiring the inverse limit technique.
\end{example}

\section{Information Systems and Approximation}
\label{sec:information-systems}

\subsection{Finitary Approximation}
\label{sec:finitary-approx}

A key insight of domain theory is that every element of a domain can be approximated by \emph{finite} (compact) elements:

\begin{definition}[Compact Element]
\label{def:compact-element}
An element $d \in D$ is \textbf{compact} (or \textbf{finite}) if for every directed set $S$ with $d \sqsubseteq \bigsqcup S$, there exists a finite subset $S_0 \subseteq S$ such that $d \sqsubseteq \bigsqcup S_0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Algebraic CPO]
\label{def:algebraic-cpo}
A CPO $D$ is \textbf{algebraic} if every element is the directed supremum of the compact elements below it:
\[
\forall x \in D.\; x = \bigsqcup \{d \in K(D) \mid d \sqsubseteq x\}
\]
where $K(D)$ is the set of compact elements of $D$.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Compact Elements of Function Spaces]
\label{ex:compact-functions}
The compact elements of $[A \to B]$ (where $A$ and $B$ are algebraic) are the \emph{step functions}: finite sets of pairs $\{(a_1, b_1), \ldots, (a_n, b_n)\}$ where each $a_i$ is compact in $A$ and each $b_i$ is compact in $B$. The step function maps $x$ to $\bigsqcup \{b_i \mid a_i \sqsubseteq x\}$.
\end{example}

\subsection{Scott Information Systems}
\label{sec:scott-info-systems}

Scott information systems provide a \emph{propositional} representation of domains:

\begin{definition}[Scott Information System]
\label{def:scott-info}
A \textbf{Scott information system} is a triple $(A, \text{Con}, \vdash)$ where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $A$ is a set of \emph{tokens} (propositions about elements)
  \item $\text{Con} \subseteq \mathcal{P}_{\text{fin}}(A)$ is a set of \emph{consistent} finite sets of tokens
  \item $\vdash \subseteq \text{Con} \times A$ is an \emph{entailment} relation
\end{itemize}
satisfying appropriate consistency conditions.
\end{definition}

The elements of the domain are the \emph{ideals}---sets of tokens that are consistent and closed under entailment. This provides a concrete, finitary way to describe domains and continuous functions.

\section{Game Semantics}
\label{sec:game-semantics}

\subsection{Games and Strategies}
\label{sec:games-strategies}

\emph{Game semantics} interprets types as games and programs as strategies:

\begin{definition}[Game]
\label{def:game}
A \textbf{game} $A$ consists of:
\begin{itemize}
  \item A set of \emph{moves} $M_A$
  \item A \textbf{precedence} relation on moves (a forest structure)
  \item A \textbf{polarization} (each move belongs to Opponent or Proponent)
  \item A set of \textbf{valid plays} (sequences of moves alternating between players)
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Strategy]
\label{def:strategy}
A \textbf{strategy} for Proponent in game $A$ is a set of even-length valid plays (positions where it is Proponent's turn) that is:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Deterministic}: For each position, there is at most one response.
  \item \textbf{Closed under prefix}: Every prefix of a play in the strategy is also in the strategy.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Full Abstraction via Game Semantics]
\label{thm:game-full-abs}
For PCF, the game semantics model is fully abstract: two terms are observationally equivalent if and only if they denote the same strategy. This was proved independently by Abramsky, Jagadeesan, and Malacaria (1994) and by Hyland and Ong (2000).
\end{theorem}

The game semantics approach has been extended to many other languages, including polymorphism, state, and concurrency, providing the most successful approach to full abstraction to date.
